.\" basename.1 -- maux's basename(1), in the V7 man(1) shape.
.\" An original page (this project's own text, not copied from a
.\" historic basename(1)), so it carries no third-party notice --
.\" MIT like the code, same posture as printf.1. Rendered at
.\" build time to build/man/BASENAME.TXT and staged on the play
.\" image as /usr/man/BASENAME: `more /usr/man/BASENAME`.
.TH BASENAME 1 "September 14, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
basename \- return the last component of a pathname
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B basename
.I string
[
.I suffix
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Basename
deletes any prefix ending in the last slash
.RB ( / )
character in
.IR string ,
after first stripping trailing slashes, and writes the result
to standard output.
.PP
If
.I string
is empty, an empty line is printed. If
.I string
consists entirely of slashes, a single
.B /
is printed.
.PP
If
.I suffix
is given, and it is not identical to the remaining characters
of
.IR string ,
and it is a trailing suffix of them, it is also removed. A
.I suffix
at least as long as the remaining characters, or an empty
.IR suffix ,
is never removed.
.PP
.I String
is text, not a filesystem lookup: no file named by
.I string
needs to exist, and none is opened.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/BASENAME  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
dirname(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 on success; 2 on a usage error (wrong number of
operands); 1 if the result would not fit basename's own working
buffer (96 bytes).
.SH BUGS
This is the POSIX single-string form only. The BSD extensions
.B \-a
(treat every operand as its own
.IR string )
and
.BI \-s " suffix"
are not implemented -- a second operand is always
.IR suffix ,
never a second
.IR string .
